<Page
  title="About"
  description="Who runs openstudy.id, why it exists, how it is funded, and the commercial relationship stated openly."
  current="/about/"
>
  <h1>Who runs this,<br /><em>and why</em></h1>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Disclosure<span>stated, not buried</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          I build analytical instruments — pipelines, models, dashboards — and I run a
          consultancy. This site is where I build them in the open, for researchers who
          have a finding and no way to make it explorable.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>The research stays theirs.</b> I take engineering credit, and I cite this work
          commercially. Nothing here is exclusive, and the code is permissively licensed.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">— Taufik Adi Nugraha</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        That is the whole arrangement. It is written here rather than inferred later,
        because the alternative is a contributor working it out for themselves after they
        have contributed — and in a professional community this size, that story travels
        further than the site would.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stated up front, the same facts are simply an offer: the studio's motive and the
        thing being offered are the same act. Demonstrating what I can build and giving a
        researcher a free instrument are not in tension.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Funding<span>and what that implies</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>One person, one server, no grant</h2>
      <p>
        Hosting and domain cost roughly two hundred dollars a year, paid personally. There
        is no funder, no institution, and no staff. The real cost is time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two consequences worth stating plainly:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>It could stop.</b> If it does, work with a DOI survives independently of
          this site. That is the main argument for taking one.</li>
        <li><b>Capacity is the constraint.</b> Instruments are built one at a time by one
          person. If the queue is long, you will be told the queue is long.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Not this<span>what it is not</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Three things this site is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Not a journal.</b> No peer review of record, and no claim to it. Validation
          gates are checking, not refereeing.</li>
        <li><b>Not a repository.</b> It does not archive datasets or host other people's
          files. Instruments fetch data from its origin.</li>
        <li><b>Not a platform.</b> There is no submission queue and no account system. It
          is a studio with a public workbench, and it will stay that way until there is a
          reason it should not be.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="aside">
        A related project — a commercial knowledge assistant — is built by the same person
        and is <em>not</em> part of this site. Keeping the two visibly separate is what lets
        this one be described honestly.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Contact<span>how to start</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Starting a conversation</h2>
      <p>
        The useful first message names the finding and the data: <em>"I published X using
        Y; can this be made explorable?"</em> That is enough to say yes, no, or "here is
        what could actually be built."
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are an engineer rather than a researcher, the smallest useful message names
        an instrument and what you think is wrong with its evaluation. See
        <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="dim">Contact details go here.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
